From 7b26e367710b6f06dad1bbc8a8b898637b531cf4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zed-patches <zed-patches@localhost>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 19:38:48 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] zed://agent?session=<id> reopens an existing thread

`zed://agent` could start a conversation but never return to one. The URL
handler accepted a single parameter, `?prompt=`, and every path through it
ended at `new_agent_thread_with_external_source_prompt` -- so a link could
seed a new thread and nothing could point at a thread that already existed.

Everything needed to do it was already here, on both sides:

  * `AgentPanel::open_thread` takes an `acp::SessionId`, resolves it through
    `ThreadMetadataStore::entry_by_session`, and falls back to loading the
    session when this machine has no row for it. Its own comment says "Share
    links / clipboard imports enter with only a session id", so entering by
    session id is a case it was written for.

  * The id is not a Zed-local handle. It is the agent session id -- for an
    ACP agent, the same id the agent itself uses. Verified against this
    machine: two session ids out of `sidebar_threads` both have a Claude Code
    transcript of the same name under ~/.claude/projects. So whatever produces
    the link -- a share link, a shell script, a tray daemon reading agent hooks
    -- needs to know nothing about Zed's internal thread ids.

What was missing was the three lines of plumbing between them. This adds
`session_id` to `OpenRequestKind::AgentPanel`, teaches `parse_agent_url` to
read `session=`, and routes a request carrying one to `open_thread`.

Two decisions worth stating, because both are silent if wrong:

  * **`session` wins over `prompt`.** A link that names a thread is asking for
    that thread; seeding a prompt into it would put words into a conversation
    the user reopened to read. They are mutually exclusive in practice, and the
    precedence has a test rather than a comment.

  * **An empty `session=` is not a session.** `?session=&prompt=hello` would
    otherwise resolve to no thread AND swallow the prompt -- the one input that
    could silently do nothing at all. It falls back to the prompt path, also
    under test.

An id this machine has never seen is not an error: `open_thread` already
handles the unknown-session case by loading it, which is what makes a link
from another host work at all.

Upstreamable as-is: it adds a parameter to an existing scheme, changes no
default, and the behaviour it exposes was already reachable in the panel.
